---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="JSON Essentials: Formatting, Validating, and Converting"
  description="A practical walkthrough of the JSON tasks developers hit most often — formatting, schema validation, and converting to and from other formats."
>
  <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 prose prose-slate dark:prose-invert">
    <p class="text-sm text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400 font-medium mb-2">Guide</p>
    <h1>JSON Essentials: Formatting, Validating, and Converting</h1>
    <p>
      JSON is the default data format for most APIs, but working with it in practice involves more
      than just reading and writing objects — minified responses need formatting to be readable,
      structures need validating before they're trusted, and data frequently needs converting to or
      from other formats. This guide covers the common tasks in the order you're likely to hit them.
    </p>

    <h2>Step 1: Make it readable</h2>
    <p>
      A minified API response — the kind you get from browser dev tools or a curl request — is a
      single dense line that's nearly impossible to scan. Run it through the
      <a href="/tools/json-formatter">JSON Formatter</a> first, always, before trying to understand
      or debug anything else about it. If you're going the other direction — reducing a formatted
      file's size for storage or transfer — the <a href="/tools/json-minifier">JSON Minifier</a>
      strips the whitespace back out.
    </p>

    <h2>Step 2: Compare or inspect specific values</h2>
    <p>
      Once readable, two common needs come up: figuring out what changed between two versions of a
      response, and extracting one specific nested value without eyeballing a deep structure. The
      <a href="/tools/json-diff">JSON Diff Checker</a> handles the first — useful for comparing an
      API response before and after a backend change. The
      <a href="/tools/json-path-tester">JSON Path Tester</a> handles the second — enter a dot-notation
      path and get the exact value back, which is faster and less error-prone than counting array
      indices by eye.
    </p>

    <h2>Step 3: Validate structure before trusting it</h2>
    <p>
      If your application depends on a JSON payload having a specific shape — required fields,
      correct types — validate it rather than assuming. The
      <a href="/tools/json-schema-generator">JSON Schema Generator</a> creates a formal schema
      definition from a sample object, which you can then use to validate future payloads
      programmatically rather than trusting each new response by default.
    </p>

    <h2>Step 4: Convert to whatever format you actually need</h2>
    <p>
      JSON rarely stays JSON for its entire lifecycle. Common conversions and when they come up:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong><a href="/tools/json-to-csv">JSON to CSV</a></strong> — exporting API data to a
        spreadsheet for someone outside engineering to review.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><a href="/tools/csv-to-json">CSV to JSON</a></strong> — the reverse, common when
        importing spreadsheet-sourced data into an application or database.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><a href="/tools/yaml-json-converter">YAML ↔ JSON</a></strong> — Kubernetes and CI/CD
        configs are usually YAML; converting to JSON makes them easier to process programmatically.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><a href="/tools/xml-json-converter">XML ↔ JSON</a></strong> — needed when integrating
        with an older or enterprise API that predates JSON's dominance.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><a href="/tools/json-to-typescript">JSON to TypeScript</a></strong> — generates
        interface definitions directly from a sample response, saving the tedium of hand-typing a
        deeply nested API response shape.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>A note on trust and validation</h2>
    <p>
      A schema generated from one sample object only reflects that sample — it won't automatically
      know that a field is sometimes null, or that an array can sometimes be empty. Treat a generated
      schema as a strong starting point, then adjust it based on your API's actual documented
      behavior across edge cases, not just the one response you happened to test with.
    </p>

    <h2>Related tools</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/tools/json-formatter">JSON Formatter</a></li>
      <li><a href="/tools/json-minifier">JSON Minifier</a></li>
      <li><a href="/tools/json-diff">JSON Diff Checker</a></li>
      <li><a href="/tools/json-path-tester">JSON Path Tester</a></li>
      <li><a href="/tools/json-schema-generator">JSON Schema Generator</a></li>
      <li><a href="/tools/json-to-typescript">JSON to TypeScript Interface</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
